An open modal needs to **trap** keyboard focus inside itself — Tab and Shift+Tab should cycle only among the modal's own focusable elements, never escaping to whatever is visually behind it, which a sighted mouse user can't reach anyway while the modal is open.

```js
function trapFocus(modalEl) {
  const focusable = modalEl.querySelectorAll(
    'a[href], button:not([disabled]), input, select, textarea, [tabindex="0"]'
  );
  const first = focusable[0];
  const last = focusable[focusable.length - 1];

  modalEl.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key !== 'Tab') return;
    if (e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === first) {
      e.preventDefault(); last.focus();
    } else if (!e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === last) {
      e.preventDefault(); first.focus();
    }
  });
}
```

This wraps focus from the last element back to the first (and vice versa on Shift+Tab) rather than letting it escape into the page behind the modal. Two details that are easy to miss and both matter: focus must move *into* the modal the moment it opens (usually to its heading or first control), and must move *back* to whatever triggered the modal when it closes — leaving focus on a now-removed trigger, or resetting it to the document body, both feel broken to a keyboard user.

A keyboard user tabbing through a page with a large navigation menu has to press Tab through every single nav link, on every single page, before reaching the actual content — a tax a sighted mouse user never pays, since they can just click or scroll straight to what they want.

A **skip link** — usually the very first focusable element on the page, visually hidden until it receives focus — lets a keyboard user bypass that entirely:

```html
<a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>
<nav>...</nav>
<main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">...</main>
```

```css
.skip-link {
  position: absolute;
  left: -9999px;
}
.skip-link:focus {
  left: 1rem;
  top: 1rem; /* becomes visible only once it receives keyboard focus */
}
```

The `tabindex="-1"` on `#main-content` is required for the jump to actually move keyboard focus there, not just scroll the page — without it, the browser scrolls to the anchor but focus stays on the skip link itself, so a screen reader user's next Tab press re-enters the navigation instead of the content they just "skipped" to.
